Scenario ID: S06
Scenario title: Vendor Meeting and Improvement Action Follow-up
Initial workflow description:
The team meets with an equipment vendor to discuss improvement ideas. After the meeting, someone summarizes the discussion and follows up on action items.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition triggers the scheduling of a vendor meeting — is it recurring on a fixed schedule, initiated by an internal request, or prompted by a specific operational event such as equipment underperformance?",
      "answer": "The meeting is triggered by an improvement request or an equipment issue summary — so it is prompted by an internal operational need rather than a fixed recurring schedule. The equipment engineer prepares the agenda and issue summary first, which then initiates the scheduling step."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "Which specific roles or job functions are expected to attend the vendor meeting, and which role is responsible for producing the post-meeting summary?",
      "answer": "The equipment engineer and vendor representative participate in discussing improvement options. The meeting organizer attends to record decisions and open questions, and is responsible for producing and sharing the meeting summary. The manager is also involved post-meeting for action-item assignment."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Is there a defined template or structured format that must be followed when writing the meeting summary, or is it created in free-form using whatever tool the author prefers?",
      "answer": "A specific template or structured format is not specified in the workflow. The meeting organizer records decisions and open questions and shares the summary via a shared document repository, but the exact format or tool used is not defined beyond that."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "How are action items captured and assigned during or after the meeting — for example, are they recorded in a spreadsheet, a project management tool, or an email thread, and who is responsible for assigning ownership and due dates?",
      "answer": "Action items are recorded in an action-item tracker and stored in a shared document repository. The manager is responsible for assigning owners and due dates after the meeting organizer has recorded the decisions and open questions."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "What method is used to follow up on open action items — are reminders sent manually, through an automated system, or via scheduled review meetings, and is there an escalation path if an item is overdue?",
      "answer": "Action-item progress is tracked by the action-item owner using the action-item tracker. Automated reminders and status tracking are identified as an automation opportunity. A missed follow-up deadline is listed as an exception, but a formal escalation path is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What criteria must be met for the entire follow-up process to be considered complete — does every action item need to be individually closed and confirmed, or is there a sign-off step by a specific role?",
      "answer": "Specific completion criteria or a formal sign-off step are not defined in the workflow. The process concludes with action-item owners tracking progress. All action items must have clearly assigned owners as a prerequisite; unclear ownership is listed as an exception condition."
    }
  ]
}
